###Pure
// order-execution -- THE JOIN BETWEEN AN ORDER AND THE TRADES IT BECAME.
//
// Layer 2. Depends on order-core (the order and the fill as the trading desk records them)
// and trade-capture (the trade as the back office records it), and on nothing else.
//
// The question this project exists to answer is the one a client actually asks: "you said
// you would buy me a million shares -- what did I pay, and could you have done better?"
// Answering it needs three things that live nowhere else:
//
//   THE LINK       An order is worked, each slice comes back as one or more FILLS, and each
//                  fill is booked as a trade. order-core owns the order and its executions;
//                  trade-capture owns the trade. Neither knows about the other. `Fill` here
//                  is the row that holds both ends -- it reaches `order_core::Order`, the
//                  `order_core::Execution` the venue reported, and (through `TradeBooking`)
//                  the `trade_capture::Trade` the fill was booked as. That is the
//                  CROSS-PROJECT ASSOCIATION this project is built around, and it crosses
//                  into BOTH dependencies.
//
//   THE SPLIT      A fill belongs to the desk; the shares belong to clients. `AllocationSet`
//                  and `FillAllocation` are the split, and the constraint that the split
//                  must add back up to the fill is on the SET, not on the pieces, because
//                  only the set knows the total.
//
//   THE NUMBER     Best execution is not an opinion. It is the fill price measured against
//                  the ARRIVAL PRICE (what the market showed when the order was accepted),
//                  against the VENUE'S OWN QUOTE at the moment of the fill, and against a
//                  benchmark over the working interval. Slippage, market impact, reversion
//                  and implementation shortfall are those measurements, each as its own row
//                  so a client can be shown the arithmetic rather than the answer.
//
// CONSTRAINTS are the second construct this project exercises, and they sit on the classes
// where the invariant is actually knowable. Every comparison is FULLY PARENTHESISED: Pure
// binds `&&` tighter than the comparison operators, so `$this.a > 0.0 && $this.a < 1.0`
// parses as `(($this.a > 0.0) && $this.a) < 1.0` and does not compile.
//
// Every relationship in this project is an ASSOCIATION, declared at the bottom of this file
// rather than as a property in a class body -- an association end already adds the property
// to both classes, and declaring it twice is a duplicate.
//
// Quantities here are Float, not the Integer that order-core uses for share counts, because
// a fill is allocated in fractions and an average is not a share count.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the link

// ONE FILL, ENRICHED. The unit of this whole project: so many shares, at this price, on
// this venue, at this microsecond, against one order -- and carrying everything the desk
// needs to say afterwards whether that was a good fill. The raw event is
// `order_core::Execution`, which the venue reported; this is the desk's own record of it,
// which is why the two are separate rows joined by `sourceExecution` rather than one.
Class order_execution::Fill
[
   // The client's two questions, as invariants. A fill of nothing is a message, not a fill.
   fillQuantityIsPositive: ($this.fillQuantity > 0.0),
   // A NEGATIVE OR ZERO PRICE IS NEVER A FILL. It is a feed error, and it must not be
   // allowed to reach an average price, where it would quietly poison every number below.
   fillPriceIsPositive: ($this.fillPrice > 0.0),
   // The gross is stored (it is what the venue sent) and recomputable. They may differ by
   // a rounding cent and no more.
   grossAgreesWithQuantityTimesPrice:
      ((($this.fillQuantity * $this.fillPrice) - $this.grossValue) < 0.01),
   // A fill cannot be reported before it happened.
   reportedAtOrAfterExecution: ($this.reportedAt >= $this.executedAt),
   currencyIsThreeLetters: ($this.currency->length() == 3)
]
{
   fillId: String[1];
   // What the venue calls it. The only handle a venue query has, so it is kept verbatim.
   venueFillRef: String[0..1];
   fillQuantity: Float[1];
   fillPrice: Float[1];
   grossValue: Float[1];
   currency: String[1];
   // Cumulative on the ORDER as at this fill, copied forward by the fill handler. Kept on
   // the fill rather than recomputed, so an out-of-sequence fill is visible as a jump.
   cumulativeQuantity: Float[1];
   executedAt: DateTime[1];
   reportedAt: DateTime[1];
   // AGGRESSIVE, PASSIVE, MIDPOINT, AUCTION -- what the fill did to the book. The single
   // most predictive field for whether the fill cost the client anything.
   liquidityFlag: String[0..1];
   // A fill that never happened, marked rather than deleted, because the client was already
   // told about it. Everything downstream of a busted fill must be ignored.
   bustTime: DateTime[0..1];
   // The MIC of the place of execution, as a plain string, because the venue object hangs
   // off the order and a fill must still be readable when it does not.
   executionVenueMic: String[0..1];
   settlementDate: StrictDate[0..1];

   // A busted fill is still a row; only the absence of a bust time makes it real.
   isLive() { $this.bustTime->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];

   // The recomputed gross, for the constraint above and for anyone who does not trust the
   // stored one. Both operands are Float, so no widening is needed here -- unlike
   // order-core's Execution, whose quantity is an Integer and must be `->toFloat()`d.
   computedGross() { $this.fillQuantity * $this.fillPrice } : Float[1];

   // A fill that took liquidity paid the spread; a fill that made it was paid for it.
   isAggressive() { $this.liquidityFlag == 'AGGRESSIVE' } : Boolean[1];
}

// THE FIX MESSAGE THAT TOLD US ABOUT THE FILL. Kept as its own row because a single fill is
// reported more than once -- an initial 35=8, then a correction, then a trade-bust -- and
// the sequence of what we were told, and when, is the only defence in a dispute.
Class order_execution::ExecutionReport
{
   reportId: String[1];
   fillId: String[1];
   // FIX ExecType: NEW, PARTIAL_FILL, FILL, TRADE_CORRECT, TRADE_CANCEL.
   execType: String[1];
   sequenceNumber: Integer[1];
   receivedAt: DateTime[1];
   fixSessionId: String[0..1];
   rawMessage: String[0..1];
   isDuplicate: Boolean[1];
}

// A CORRECTION TO A FILL AFTER IT WAS PUBLISHED -- the venue changed the price, the size, or
// busted it outright. The old values are on this row and not overwritten on the fill,
// because the client was invoiced off the old ones.
Class order_execution::FillAmendment
[
   amendedQuantityIsPositive: ($this.newQuantity > 0.0),
   amendedPriceIsPositive: ($this.newPrice > 0.0)
]
{
   amendmentId: String[1];
   fillId: String[1];
   amendmentReason: String[1];
   oldQuantity: Float[1];
   newQuantity: Float[1];
   oldPrice: Float[1];
   newPrice: Float[1];
   amendedAt: DateTime[1];
   amendedBy: String[1];
   isVenueInitiated: Boolean[1];

   // What the correction moved, signed. A large one is a conversation with the venue.
   quantityDelta() { $this.newQuantity - $this.oldQuantity } : Float[1];
   priceDelta() { $this.newPrice - $this.oldPrice } : Float[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the split

// THE COMPLETE SPLIT OF ONE FILL ACROSS CLIENT ACCOUNTS, as one row. It exists so that "the
// allocations add up" has somewhere to be TRUE: an individual allocation cannot know the
// total, and the fill cannot know whether the last piece has arrived yet. This is the row a
// client-money control reads.
Class order_execution::AllocationSet
[
   // THE INVARIANT THIS CLASS EXISTS FOR: the pieces sum to the whole. Written as two
   // one-sided comparisons rather than an absolute difference, each fully parenthesised
   // inside the `&&`, because `&&` binds tighter than `<`.
   allocatedQuantitySumsToFill:
      ((($this.allocatedQuantity - $this.fillQuantity) < 0.000001) &&
       (($this.fillQuantity - $this.allocatedQuantity) < 0.000001)),
   allocationCountIsPositive: ($this.allocationCount > 0),
   setFillQuantityIsPositive: ($this.fillQuantity > 0.0)
]
{
   allocationSetId: String[1];
   fillId: String[1];
   // The fill's quantity, copied here so the invariant is checkable on one row.
   fillQuantity: Float[1];
   // The sum of the pieces, maintained by the allocation engine.
   allocatedQuantity: Float[1];
   allocationCount: Integer[1];
   allocationStatus: String[1];
   allocatedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   allocatedBy: String[0..1];
   // The rule that produced the split: PRO_RATA, PRIORITY, RANDOM, MANUAL. Two clients who
   // both wanted the whole fill will ask which one was used.
   allocationMethod: String[1];

   // What has not been given to anybody yet. Non-zero on a set that claims to be complete
   // is exactly the break the class below records.
   unallocatedQuantity() { $this.fillQuantity - $this.allocatedQuantity } : Float[1];

   isFullyAllocated() { $this.allocationStatus == 'ALLOCATED' } : Boolean[1];
}

// ONE ACCOUNT'S SHARE OF ONE FILL. The account is held as a plain string reference:
// core-account is not a dependency of this project, and inventing a dependency to hold an
// identifier would be the undeclared-dependency defect this graph exists to detect.
Class order_execution::FillAllocation
[
   allocatedQuantityIsPositive: ($this.allocatedQuantity > 0.0),
   allocationPriceIsPositive: ($this.allocationPrice > 0.0),
   // A share of the fill cannot be more than the fill.
   allocatedNotAboveFill: ($this.allocatedQuantity <= $this.fillQuantity)
]
{
   fillAllocationId: String[1];
   allocationSetId: String[1];
   accountRef: String[1];
   clientRef: String[0..1];
   allocatedQuantity: Float[1];
   // The fill quantity, carried so the piece can be checked against the whole without a
   // navigation. Denormalised on purpose.
   fillQuantity: Float[1];
   // Usually the fill price, but an averaged allocation across several fills is not.
   allocationPrice: Float[1];
   allocationCurrency: String[1];
   allocationStatus: String[1];
   allocatedAt: DateTime[0..1];

   // `/` widens to Float even on two Integers, so a dividing derived property is Float
   // whatever it divides. Here both sides are already Float.
   shareOfFill() { $this.allocatedQuantity / $this.fillQuantity } : Float[1];

   consideration() { $this.allocatedQuantity * $this.allocationPrice } : Float[1];
}

// A SPLIT THAT DOES NOT ADD UP, as a row someone has to clear. The class constraint on
// AllocationSet says what must be true; this says what to do when it is not, which is a
// different job and needs a name, an owner and a date.
Class order_execution::AllocationBreak
{
   allocationBreakId: String[1];
   allocationSetId: String[1];
   // SHORT, OVER, PRICE_MISMATCH, ACCOUNT_UNKNOWN.
   breakType: String[1];
   breakQuantity: Float[0..1];
   breakAmount: Float[0..1];
   severity: String[1];
   detectedAt: DateTime[1];
   resolvedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   assignedTo: String[0..1];
   resolutionNote: String[0..1];

   isOpen() { $this.resolvedAt->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ order-level roll-up

// ONE ORDER'S FILLS, TOTALLED. The desk blotter line: how much of the order is done, at what
// average, over how many fills and how many venues. It carries the order's own quantity as a
// snapshot so the one invariant that matters at this level -- YOU CANNOT FILL MORE THAN YOU
// WERE ASKED TO BUY -- is checkable on a single row.
Class order_execution::OrderFillSummary
[
   // THE OVERFILL CHECK. An order filled beyond its quantity is a position the client did
   // not ask for and the desk owns.
   filledQuantityNotAboveOrderQuantity: ($this.filledQuantity <= $this.orderQuantity),
   orderQuantityIsPositive: ($this.orderQuantity > 0.0),
   filledQuantityIsNotNegative: ($this.filledQuantity >= 0.0),
   averagePriceIsPositive: ($this.averagePrice > 0.0),
   fillCountIsNotNegative: ($this.fillCount >= 0)
]
{
   summaryId: String[1];
   orderId: String[1];
   // The order's quantity as at this summary. Snapshotted, because an order that is resized
   // after the fact must not retrospectively make an old overfill legal.
   orderQuantity: Float[1];
   filledQuantity: Float[1];
   averagePrice: Float[1];
   grossConsideration: Float[1];
   currency: String[1];
   fillCount: Integer[1];
   venueCount: Integer[1];
   firstFillAt: DateTime[0..1];
   lastFillAt: DateTime[0..1];
   summaryAsOf: DateTime[1];

   // How much of the order is done. Both operands are Float here, but the return type would
   // be Float either way, because `/` widens.
   fillRate() { $this.filledQuantity / $this.orderQuantity } : Float[1];

   // What is still to be worked. Never negative if the constraint above holds -- which is
   // the point of having the constraint.
   unfilledQuantity() { $this.orderQuantity - $this.filledQuantity } : Float[1];

   isComplete() { $this.filledQuantity >= $this.orderQuantity } : Boolean[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ into trade-capture

// THE RECORD THAT A FILL BECAME A TRADE. This is the seam between the front office and the
// back office and it is deliberately a ROW, not a foreign key on the fill: booking can fail,
// can be retried, and can produce a trade whose economics differ from the fill's, and every
// one of those is something an operations team has to see.
Class order_execution::TradeBooking
[
   bookedQuantityIsPositive: ($this.bookedQuantity > 0.0),
   bookedPriceIsPositive: ($this.bookedPrice > 0.0),
   // What was booked cannot exceed what was filled.
   bookedNotAboveFilled: ($this.bookedQuantity <= $this.fillQuantity)
]
{
   bookingId: String[1];
   fillId: String[1];
   // The trade-capture trade reference, kept as a string as well as reached by association,
   // because the booking row must still name its trade when the trade has not landed yet.
   tradeRef: String[0..1];
   bookingStatus: String[1];
   // The fill's quantity and what was actually booked, side by side, so the two can be
   // compared without leaving the row.
   fillQuantity: Float[1];
   bookedQuantity: Float[1];
   bookedPrice: Float[1];
   bookedCurrency: String[1];
   bookedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   bookingAttempts: Integer[1];
   rejectionReason: String[0..1];
   isAutoBooked: Boolean[1];

   // Booked, and booked in full. A partial booking is a break waiting to be raised.
   isBooked() { $this.bookingStatus == 'BOOKED' } : Boolean[1];

   unbookedQuantity() { $this.fillQuantity - $this.bookedQuantity } : Float[1];
}

// ONE FIELD ON WHICH THE FILL AND THE BOOKED TRADE DISAGREE. One row per field, not one per
// booking, because "the quantity is wrong" and "the settlement date is wrong" go to
// different people.
Class order_execution::BookingBreak
{
   bookingBreakId: String[1];
   bookingId: String[1];
   fieldName: String[1];
   fillValue: String[0..1];
   tradeValue: String[0..1];
   breakType: String[1];
   severity: String[1];
   detectedAt: DateTime[1];
   resolvedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   assignedTo: String[0..1];

   isOpen() { $this.resolvedAt->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// ONE MONEY LINE ON A FILL. Explicit costs (commission, exchange fee, clearing) have an
// invoice behind them; implicit ones (spread, impact, delay) do not, and the difference is
// the whole argument about what an execution really cost. Both are rows here, flagged.
Class order_execution::ExecutionCostLine
[
   costAmountIsNotNegative: ($this.costAmount >= 0.0),
   costBasisPointsIsNotNegative: ($this.costBasisPoints >= 0.0)
]
{
   costLineId: String[1];
   fillId: String[1];
   // COMMISSION, EXCHANGE_FEE, CLEARING_FEE, SPREAD_COST, IMPACT_COST, DELAY_COST.
   costType: String[1];
   // The line that separates an invoice from an estimate.
   isExplicit: Boolean[1];
   costAmount: Float[1];
   costCurrency: String[1];
   costBasisPoints: Float[1];
   // Whether the client pays it or the desk absorbs it.
   isChargedToClient: Boolean[1];
   calculationMethod: String[0..1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the benchmark

// THE MARKET AT THE MOMENT THE ORDER WAS ACCEPTED. Everything a client is told about
// execution quality is measured from here, so this row is taken once, on arrival, and never
// updated -- a benchmark that moves is not a benchmark.
Class order_execution::ArrivalSnapshot
[
   arrivalPriceIsPositive: ($this.arrivalPrice > 0.0),
   // A crossed book is a bad snapshot, and a bad snapshot poisons every number below it.
   askIsNotBelowBid: ($this.askPrice >= $this.bidPrice),
   bidIsPositive: ($this.bidPrice > 0.0)
]
{
   snapshotId: String[1];
   orderId: String[1];
   // The mid, usually, but a market order in a wide book may be snapped on the far touch.
   arrivalPrice: Float[1];
   bidPrice: Float[1];
   askPrice: Float[1];
   bidSize: Float[0..1];
   askSize: Float[0..1];
   lastTradedPrice: Float[0..1];
   snapshotCurrency: String[1];
   arrivedAt: DateTime[1];
   sourceFeed: String[1];
   // The MIC the snapshot was taken from, which need not be where the order was filled.
   snapshotVenueMic: String[0..1];

   // The cost of crossing, in price terms. Half of it is what an aggressive fill pays.
   spread() { $this.askPrice - $this.bidPrice } : Float[1];

   // The mid recomputed from the touch. Compared against arrivalPrice it says which
   // convention the snapshotting process used.
   midPrice() { ($this.bidPrice + $this.askPrice) / 2.0 } : Float[1];

   // The spread as the market quotes it: basis points of the mid.
   spreadBasisPoints()
   {
      (($this.askPrice - $this.bidPrice) / (($this.bidPrice + $this.askPrice) / 2.0)) * 10000.0
   } : Float[1];
}

// ONE BENCHMARK VALUE FOR ONE ORDER OVER ONE INTERVAL -- interval VWAP, TWAP, the open, the
// close, the arrival price. Several per order, because a client who is shown only the
// benchmark that flatters the desk will eventually ask for the others.
Class order_execution::ExecutionBenchmark
[
   benchmarkPriceIsPositive: ($this.benchmarkPrice > 0.0),
   intervalEndsAfterItStarts: ($this.intervalEnd >= $this.intervalStart)
]
{
   benchmarkId: String[1];
   orderId: String[1];
   // ARRIVAL, INTERVAL_VWAP, FULL_DAY_VWAP, TWAP, OPEN, CLOSE, PARTICIPATION_WEIGHTED.
   benchmarkType: String[1];
   benchmarkPrice: Float[1];
   benchmarkCurrency: String[1];
   intervalStart: DateTime[1];
   intervalEnd: DateTime[1];
   // The volume the benchmark was computed over; a VWAP on thin volume is not evidence.
   benchmarkVolume: Float[0..1];
   sourceFeed: String[1];
   isPrimary: Boolean[1];
}

// ONE FILL MEASURED AGAINST ONE BENCHMARK. Signed in the client's favour: a negative number
// means the fill beat the benchmark, whichever side the order was. One row per (fill,
// benchmark) pair, which is why this is a class and not two columns on the fill.
Class order_execution::SlippageMeasure
[
   slippageFillPriceIsPositive: ($this.fillPrice > 0.0),
   slippageBenchmarkPriceIsPositive: ($this.benchmarkPrice > 0.0),
   measuredQuantityIsPositive: ($this.measuredQuantity > 0.0)
]
{
   slippageId: String[1];
   fillId: String[1];
   benchmarkRef: String[0..1];
   benchmarkType: String[1];
   fillPrice: Float[1];
   benchmarkPrice: Float[1];
   measuredQuantity: Float[1];
   // Signed cost in currency, and the same thing in basis points, both stored because a
   // client report shows one and a desk report shows the other.
   slippageAmount: Float[1];
   slippageBasisPoints: Float[1];
   slippageCurrency: String[1];
   // +1 for a buy, -1 for a sell; the multiplier that makes the sign mean "cost".
   sideSign: Float[1];
   measuredAt: DateTime[1];

   // The measurement, recomputed. Signed so that positive is always a cost to the client.
   computedSlippage() { ($this.fillPrice - $this.benchmarkPrice) * $this.sideSign } : Float[1];

   // The same in basis points of the benchmark.
   computedBasisPoints()
   {
      ((($this.fillPrice - $this.benchmarkPrice) * $this.sideSign) / $this.benchmarkPrice) * 10000.0
   } : Float[1];

   // Beat the benchmark. The only phrasing a client ever remembers.
   isFavourable() { $this.slippageBasisPoints < 0.0 } : Boolean[1];
}

// WHAT THE ORDER ITSELF DID TO THE PRICE. Split into TEMPORARY impact (the price moved while
// we were buying and came back afterwards -- we paid it, nobody kept it) and PERMANENT
// impact (the price moved and stayed, because the trade carried information). The split
// matters: only the temporary half is avoidable by trading more slowly.
Class order_execution::MarketImpactEstimate
[
   preTradePriceIsPositive: ($this.preTradePrice > 0.0),
   postTradePriceIsPositive: ($this.postTradePrice > 0.0)
]
{
   impactId: String[1];
   fillId: String[1];
   modelName: String[1];
   preTradePrice: Float[1];
   postTradePrice: Float[1];
   temporaryImpactBasisPoints: Float[1];
   permanentImpactBasisPoints: Float[1];
   // What the model said it would be, before the fill. Kept next to the outcome, because a
   // model whose predictions are never compared to reality is decoration.
   predictedImpactBasisPoints: Float[0..1];
   impactCurrency: String[1];
   estimatedAt: DateTime[1];

   // The whole move, both halves together.
   totalImpactBasisPoints()
   {
      $this.temporaryImpactBasisPoints + $this.permanentImpactBasisPoints
   } : Float[1];

   // Model error, signed. Persistently positive means the model is optimistic.
   modelError()
   {
      if ($this.predictedImpactBasisPoints->isEmpty(),
          | 0.0,
          | ($this.temporaryImpactBasisPoints + $this.permanentImpactBasisPoints)
            - $this.predictedImpactBasisPoints->toOne())
   } : Float[1];
}

// HOW FAR THE PRICE CAME BACK AFTER THE FILL, at one horizon. Several per impact estimate --
// one minute, five minutes, the close -- because reversion at one minute and reversion at
// the close are different claims about whether the trade carried information.
Class order_execution::ReversionMeasure
[
   horizonIsPositive: ($this.horizonSeconds > 0),
   referencePriceIsPositive: ($this.referencePrice > 0.0)
]
{
   reversionId: String[1];
   impactId: String[1];
   horizonSeconds: Integer[1];
   referencePrice: Float[1];
   horizonPrice: Float[1];
   reversionBasisPoints: Float[1];
   // The move in the wider market over the same window, so a reversion that is really just
   // the index going up is not claimed as skill.
   marketMoveBasisPoints: Float[0..1];
   measuredAt: DateTime[1];

   // Reversion net of the market's own move. This is the number worth reporting.
   excessReversionBasisPoints()
   {
      if ($this.marketMoveBasisPoints->isEmpty(),
          | $this.reversionBasisPoints,
          | $this.reversionBasisPoints - $this.marketMoveBasisPoints->toOne())
   } : Float[1];
}

// THE FULL DECOMPOSITION OF WHAT THE ORDER COST, from the decision price to the last fill:
// DELAY (the price moved between deciding and starting), EXECUTION (what the fills cost
// against arrival), OPPORTUNITY (what the unfilled remainder would have cost or saved), and
// the explicit fees. One per order. This is the number in the quarterly review.
Class order_execution::ImplementationShortfall
[
   decisionPriceIsPositive: ($this.decisionPrice > 0.0),
   shortfallArrivalPriceIsPositive: ($this.arrivalPrice > 0.0),
   averageFillPriceIsPositive: ($this.averageFillPrice > 0.0),
   // The four components are stored AND summed; they may not disagree with the total.
   componentsAgreeWithTotal:
      (((($this.delayCostBasisPoints + $this.executionCostBasisPoints)
         + ($this.opportunityCostBasisPoints + $this.explicitCostBasisPoints))
        - $this.totalShortfallBasisPoints) < 0.01)
]
{
   shortfallId: String[1];
   orderId: String[1];
   // Where the portfolio manager decided, which is earlier than where the desk started.
   decisionPrice: Float[1];
   arrivalPrice: Float[1];
   averageFillPrice: Float[1];
   // Absent when the order was fully filled, which is when opportunity cost is zero.
   closingPrice: Float[0..1];
   filledQuantity: Float[1];
   unfilledQuantity: Float[1];
   delayCostBasisPoints: Float[1];
   executionCostBasisPoints: Float[1];
   opportunityCostBasisPoints: Float[1];
   explicitCostBasisPoints: Float[1];
   totalShortfallBasisPoints: Float[1];
   shortfallCurrency: String[1];
   computedAt: DateTime[1];

   // The components, resummed. The constraint above holds this against the stored total.
   computedTotalBasisPoints()
   {
      ($this.delayCostBasisPoints + $this.executionCostBasisPoints)
      + ($this.opportunityCostBasisPoints + $this.explicitCostBasisPoints)
   } : Float[1];

   totalQuantity() { $this.filledQuantity + $this.unfilledQuantity } : Float[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the venue's own quote

// WHAT THE VENUE WAS SHOWING AT THE MOMENT OF THE FILL. Best execution against the venue's
// own quote is the one test a venue cannot argue with: it published these two numbers, and
// the fill either was or was not inside them.
Class order_execution::VenueQuote
[
   quoteAskIsNotBelowBid: ($this.askPrice >= $this.bidPrice),
   quoteBidIsPositive: ($this.bidPrice > 0.0)
]
{
   quoteId: String[1];
   venueMic: String[1];
   instrumentRef: String[1];
   bidPrice: Float[1];
   askPrice: Float[1];
   bidSize: Float[1];
   askSize: Float[1];
   quotedAt: DateTime[1];
   quoteCurrency: String[1];
   // Whether this is the venue's own top of book or a consolidated tape. A comparison
   // against the wrong one is the commonest mistake in a best-execution report.
   isConsolidated: Boolean[1];
   sourceFeed: String[1];

   spread() { $this.askPrice - $this.bidPrice } : Float[1];
   midPrice() { ($this.bidPrice + $this.askPrice) / 2.0 } : Float[1];

   // A quote with nothing on one side is not a two-sided market and must not be used as a
   // benchmark. `||` compiles perfectly well; the project that reported otherwise had a
   // different fault in the same expression.
   isOneSided() { ($this.bidSize == 0.0) || ($this.askSize == 0.0) } : Boolean[1];
}

// THE FILL PRICE PUT NEXT TO THE QUOTE THAT WAS STANDING. Its own row rather than a derived
// property on the fill, because the answer depends on WHICH quote you use, and a client who
// disagrees with the verdict is really disagreeing with that choice -- which must therefore
// be recorded.
Class order_execution::QuoteComparison
[
   comparedFillPriceIsPositive: ($this.fillPrice > 0.0),
   comparedBidIsPositive: ($this.quoteBidPrice > 0.0),
   comparedAskIsNotBelowBid: ($this.quoteAskPrice >= $this.quoteBidPrice)
]
{
   comparisonId: String[1];
   fillId: String[1];
   quoteRef: String[0..1];
   fillPrice: Float[1];
   quoteBidPrice: Float[1];
   quoteAskPrice: Float[1];
   // INSIDE, AT_TOUCH, OUTSIDE, AT_MID -- the verdict, as a word, because that is what goes
   // on the report.
   priceOutcome: String[1];
   // Improvement over the touch on the client's side, in price and in basis points.
   priceImprovement: Float[1];
   priceImprovementBasisPoints: Float[1];
   // Milliseconds between the quote and the fill. A comparison against a stale quote is
   // worthless, and this is the field that says so.
   quoteAgeMillis: Integer[1];
   comparedAt: DateTime[1];

   quotedSpread() { $this.quoteAskPrice - $this.quoteBidPrice } : Float[1];

   // The comparison is only evidence if the quote was current. Half a second is generous.
   isStaleQuote() { $this.quoteAgeMillis > 500 } : Boolean[1];

   // Filled better than the touch.
   isPriceImproved() { $this.priceImprovement > 0.0 } : Boolean[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the verdict

// THE FIRM'S BEST-EXECUTION POLICY, as a version. An assessment is only meaningful against
// the policy in force when the order was worked, so the policy is a row with dates and the
// assessment points at one.
Class order_execution::ExecutionPolicy
[
   policyVersionIsPositive: ($this.policyVersion > 0),
   passThresholdInRange: (($this.passThreshold >= 0.0) && ($this.passThreshold <= 100.0))
]
{
   policyId: String[1];
   policyName: String[1];
   policyVersion: Integer[1];
   clientCategory: String[1];
   effectiveFrom: StrictDate[1];
   effectiveTo: StrictDate[0..1];
   // The score at or above which an order passes.
   passThreshold: Float[1];
   approvedBy: String[1];

   isCurrent() { $this.effectiveTo->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// THE VERDICT ON ONE ORDER: did we obtain the best result reasonably available? One row per
// order, carrying the score, the outcome and -- when the outcome is a fail -- who reviewed
// it. The reasoning lives in the factor rows below, because "we scored 61" is not an answer.
Class order_execution::BestExecutionAssessment
[
   overallScoreInRange: (($this.overallScore >= 0.0) && ($this.overallScore <= 100.0)),
   assessedQuantityIsPositive: ($this.assessedQuantity > 0.0),
   assessedAveragePriceIsPositive: ($this.averageFillPrice > 0.0)
]
{
   assessmentId: String[1];
   orderId: String[1];
   policyRef: String[0..1];
   assessedQuantity: Float[1];
   averageFillPrice: Float[1];
   arrivalPrice: Float[1];
   // Signed against arrival, in basis points; the headline number.
   slippageVsArrivalBasisPoints: Float[1];
   overallScore: Float[1];
   // PASS, PASS_WITH_COMMENT, FAIL, NOT_ASSESSED.
   outcome: String[1];
   assessedAt: DateTime[1];
   assessedBy: String[1];
   reviewedBy: String[0..1];
   reviewNote: String[0..1];

   isPass() { ($this.outcome == 'PASS') || ($this.outcome == 'PASS_WITH_COMMENT') } : Boolean[1];

   // A failed assessment nobody has looked at is the thing a regulator finds.
   isReviewed() { $this.reviewedBy->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// ONE OF THE FACTORS THE POLICY REQUIRES TO BE CONSIDERED -- price, cost, speed, likelihood
// of execution and settlement, size, nature. Each scored and weighted, so the overall score
// on the assessment can be shown to be arithmetic rather than judgement.
Class order_execution::BestExecutionFactor
[
   factorScoreInRange: (($this.factorScore >= 0.0) && ($this.factorScore <= 100.0)),
   factorWeightInRange: (($this.factorWeight >= 0.0) && ($this.factorWeight <= 1.0))
]
{
   factorId: String[1];
   assessmentId: String[1];
   // PRICE, COST, SPEED, LIKELIHOOD, SIZE, NATURE.
   factorName: String[1];
   factorScore: Float[1];
   factorWeight: Float[1];
   // What the score was read off: the measured value and its unit.
   measuredValue: Float[0..1];
   measureUnit: String[0..1];
   commentary: String[0..1];

   // The factor's contribution to the overall score. Both operands are Float -- note that
   // had the score been an Integer, `Integer * Float` types as `Number`, which is not a
   // subtype of Float, and would have needed an explicit `->toFloat()`.
   weightedScore() { $this.factorScore * $this.factorWeight } : Float[1];
}

// THE PERIODIC REPORT THE CLIENT ACTUALLY RECEIVES, gathering a period's assessments and
// venue statistics into one published document. It is a row because publication has a date,
// a version and an author, and a report that was sent cannot be silently rewritten.
Class order_execution::BestExecutionReport
[
   periodEndsAfterItStarts: ($this.periodEnd >= $this.periodStart),
   reportOrderCountIsNotNegative: ($this.orderCount >= 0)
]
{
   reportId: String[1];
   reportName: String[1];
   clientRef: String[0..1];
   // MONTHLY, QUARTERLY, ANNUAL, AD_HOC.
   reportPeriodType: String[1];
   periodStart: StrictDate[1];
   periodEnd: StrictDate[1];
   orderCount: Integer[1];
   assessedCount: Integer[1];
   failCount: Integer[1];
   publishedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   publishedBy: String[0..1];
   reportVersion: Integer[1];

   isPublished() { $this.publishedAt->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];

   // Share of the period's orders that were assessed at all. `/` widens two Integers to
   // Float, so this must be declared Float even though both operands are Integer.
   assessedRate() { $this.assessedCount / $this.orderCount } : Float[1];
}

// ONE VENUE'S EXECUTION QUALITY OVER ONE PERIOD -- the RTS 27-shaped row. It is what a desk
// uses to decide where to route next quarter, and what a client uses to ask why the desk did
// not.
Class order_execution::VenuePerformance
[
   performanceFilledQuantityIsNotNegative: ($this.filledQuantity >= 0.0),
   fillRateInRange: (($this.fillRate >= 0.0) && ($this.fillRate <= 1.0)),
   performancePeriodEndsAfterItStarts: ($this.periodEnd >= $this.periodStart)
]
{
   performanceId: String[1];
   venueMic: String[1];
   periodStart: StrictDate[1];
   periodEnd: StrictDate[1];
   orderCount: Integer[1];
   fillCount: Integer[1];
   filledQuantity: Float[1];
   notionalTraded: Float[1];
   performanceCurrency: String[1];
   fillRate: Float[1];
   averageSlippageBasisPoints: Float[1];
   averageSpreadBasisPoints: Float[1];
   priceImprovementRate: Float[1];
   averageLatencyMillis: Integer[0..1];
   rejectRate: Float[1];

   // Average trade size over the period. Float / Integer, and `/` widens either way.
   averageFillSize() { $this.filledQuantity / $this.fillCount } : Float[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ context

// HOW MUCH OF THE MARKET WE WERE, over one interval. Participation is the lever a client
// controls -- go faster and pay impact, go slower and take risk -- so what was asked for and
// what was achieved are both on the row.
Class order_execution::ParticipationMeasure
[
   targetRateInRange: (($this.targetRate >= 0.0) && ($this.targetRate <= 1.0)),
   achievedRateIsNotNegative: ($this.achievedRate >= 0.0),
   marketVolumeIsPositive: ($this.marketVolume > 0.0)
]
{
   participationId: String[1];
   orderId: String[1];
   intervalStart: DateTime[1];
   intervalEnd: DateTime[1];
   // What the algorithm was told to do, and what it managed.
   targetRate: Float[1];
   achievedRate: Float[1];
   ourVolume: Float[1];
   marketVolume: Float[1];
   intervalVwap: Float[0..1];

   // The rate, recomputed from the two volumes. Compared against achievedRate it says
   // whether the market volume feed and the fill feed agree.
   computedRate() { $this.ourVolume / $this.marketVolume } : Float[1];

   // Ahead of schedule, which is where impact comes from.
   isOverParticipating() { $this.achievedRate > $this.targetRate } : Boolean[1];
}

// WHAT THE FILL DID TO THE BOOK, split three ways: liquidity taken, liquidity added, and
// filled at the midpoint. A desk that adds liquidity is paid a rebate and pays no spread, so
// this breakdown is most of the difference between two desks' costs.
Class order_execution::LiquidityCapture
[
   capturedQuantityIsPositive: ($this.capturedQuantity > 0.0),
   spreadCaptureInRange: (($this.spreadCaptureRatio >= -2.0) && ($this.spreadCaptureRatio <= 2.0))
]
{
   captureId: String[1];
   fillId: String[1];
   capturedQuantity: Float[1];
   // PASSIVE, AGGRESSIVE, MIDPOINT, AUCTION, DARK.
   captureType: String[1];
   // Share of the spread earned (positive) or paid (negative). A passive fill at the bid on
   // a buy earns half the spread; an aggressive one pays it.
   spreadCaptureRatio: Float[1];
   spreadAtFill: Float[1];
   // Positive when the venue paid us to provide liquidity.
   rebateAmount: Float[0..1];
   rebateCurrency: String[0..1];
   // Was there a resting order of ours that this fill hit? A wash is a control failure.
   isWashRisk: Boolean[1];

   isPassive() { $this.captureType == 'PASSIVE' } : Boolean[1];

   // Money value of the spread we captured or conceded on this fill.
   spreadValue() { ($this.spreadAtFill * $this.spreadCaptureRatio) * $this.capturedQuantity } : Float[1];
}

// THE TRADING SESSION A FILL HAPPENED IN -- opening auction, continuous, closing auction,
// after hours. A fill in an auction is not comparable with a fill in continuous trading, and
// a slippage number that mixes them is misleading rather than merely imprecise.
Class order_execution::ExecutionSession
[
   sessionEndsAfterItStarts: ($this.sessionEnd >= $this.sessionStart)
]
{
   sessionId: String[1];
   venueMic: String[1];
   tradingDate: StrictDate[1];
   // OPENING_AUCTION, CONTINUOUS, INTRADAY_AUCTION, CLOSING_AUCTION, AFTER_HOURS.
   sessionType: String[1];
   sessionStart: DateTime[1];
   sessionEnd: DateTime[1];
   // Whether the book was displayed. A halt inside the session is its own field because a
   // fill during a halt needs explaining.
   isHalted: Boolean[1];
   haltReason: String[0..1];
   referencePrice: Float[0..1];

   isAuction()
   {
      ($this.sessionType == 'OPENING_AUCTION') || ($this.sessionType == 'CLOSING_AUCTION')
   } : Boolean[1];
}

// HOW LONG THE ROUND TRIP TOOK, leg by leg: our gateway out, the venue's acknowledgement,
// the fill, the report back. Latency is a best-execution factor in its own right, and it is
// the one a venue will dispute, so each leg is timed separately.
Class order_execution::LatencyMeasure
[
   totalLatencyIsNotNegative: ($this.totalLatencyMicros >= 0),
   venueLatencyIsNotNegative: ($this.venueLatencyMicros >= 0)
]
{
   latencyId: String[1];
   fillId: String[1];
   orderSentAt: DateTime[1];
   venueAckAt: DateTime[0..1];
   filledAt: DateTime[1];
   reportReceivedAt: DateTime[1];
   // Our side of the wire, the venue's side, and the whole thing.
   gatewayLatencyMicros: Integer[1];
   venueLatencyMicros: Integer[1];
   totalLatencyMicros: Integer[1];
   // Whether our clock and the venue's were disciplined to the same source. Without this,
   // none of the numbers above is admissible.
   isClockSynchronised: Boolean[1];

   // Milliseconds, for a report a human reads. `/` widens, so this is Float[1] and could
   // not be declared Integer[1].
   totalLatencyMillis() { $this.totalLatencyMicros / 1000.0 } : Float[1];

   isSlow() { $this.totalLatencyMicros > 50000 } : Boolean[1];
}

// SOMETHING THAT WENT WRONG AROUND A FILL and was noticed: a price outside the day's range,
// a fill on a restricted name, a quantity that overfills the order, a booking that will not
// go. Raised automatically, cleared by a person, and kept afterwards.
Class order_execution::ExecutionException
{
   exceptionId: String[1];
   fillId: String[1];
   // OVERFILL, PRICE_OUTLIER, RESTRICTED_INSTRUMENT, LATE_REPORT, BOOKING_FAILURE,
   // ALLOCATION_MISMATCH, STALE_QUOTE.
   exceptionType: String[1];
   severity: String[1];
   exceptionMessage: String[1];
   // What the rule looked at and what it expected, so the alert can be judged without
   // rerunning it.
   observedValue: Float[0..1];
   thresholdValue: Float[0..1];
   raisedAt: DateTime[1];
   raisedBy: String[1];
   acknowledgedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   clearedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   clearedBy: String[0..1];

   isOpen() { $this.clearedAt->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];

   // Raised, seen, and still not dealt with -- the queue an operations lead works from.
   isAcknowledgedButOpen()
   {
      ($this.acknowledgedAt->isNotEmpty()) && ($this.clearedAt->isEmpty())
   } : Boolean[1];
}

// WHAT THE CLIENT TOLD US ABOUT HOW TO WORK THE ORDER -- a participation cap, a limit, a
// venue exclusion, "work with care", "not held". A specific instruction overrides the firm's
// own policy, and best execution is then measured against the instruction rather than
// against the policy, which is why these are rows and not free text on the order.
Class order_execution::ClientExecutionInstruction
{
   instructionId: String[1];
   orderId: String[1];
   // PARTICIPATION_CAP, PRICE_LIMIT, VENUE_INCLUDE, VENUE_EXCLUDE, WORK_WITH_CARE,
   // NOT_HELD, DO_NOT_CROSS.
   instructionType: String[1];
   instructionText: String[1];
   numericValue: Float[0..1];
   venueMic: String[0..1];
   receivedAt: DateTime[1];
   receivedFrom: String[1];
   // The channel it arrived on, because a verbal instruction and a FIX tag carry different
   // evidential weight.
   receivedVia: String[1];
   // A specific instruction displaces the policy, and the client must be told so.
   overridesPolicy: Boolean[1];
   acknowledgedAt: DateTime[0..1];

   isAcknowledged() { $this.acknowledgedAt->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ associations
//
// ELEVEN of these cross into order-core and FIVE into trade-capture. Every end name added to
// a class this project does not own is chosen so it cannot collide: trade-capture has
// already put `trades`, `instrumentLegs`, `bookedTrades`, `counterpartyRoles`,
// `entityAllocations` and `brokerRoles` onto classes it does not own, and order-core put
// `instrumentOrders`, `bookOrders`, `venueOrders`, `venueExecutions` and the rest onto
// theirs. A second property of the same name on the same class fails the whole graph, so the
// names below are deliberately unlike any of them.

// ---- crossing into order-core ----

// THE CENTRAL LINK. An order is filled by many fills; a fill belongs to one order.
Association order_execution::FillOrder
{
   filledOrder: order_core::Order[0..1];
   orderFills: order_execution::Fill[*];
}

// The venue's raw execution report as order-core recorded it, and this project's enriched
// record of the same event. A fill without one came from a manual booking.
Association order_execution::FillSourceExecution
{
   sourceExecution: order_core::Execution[0..1];
   executionFill: order_execution::Fill[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::SummaryOrder
{
   summarisedOrder: order_core::Order[0..1];
   fillSummary: order_execution::OrderFillSummary[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::ArrivalOrder
{
   arrivalOrder: order_core::Order[0..1];
   arrivalSnapshot: order_execution::ArrivalSnapshot[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::BenchmarkOrder
{
   benchmarkedOrder: order_core::Order[0..1];
   executionBenchmarks: order_execution::ExecutionBenchmark[*];
}

Association order_execution::ShortfallOrder
{
   shortfallOrder: order_core::Order[0..1];
   implementationShortfall: order_execution::ImplementationShortfall[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::AssessmentOrder
{
   assessedOrder: order_core::Order[0..1];
   bestExecAssessment: order_execution::BestExecutionAssessment[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::ParticipationOrder
{
   participationOrder: order_core::Order[0..1];
   participationMeasures: order_execution::ParticipationMeasure[*];
}

Association order_execution::InstructionOrder
{
   instructedOrder: order_core::Order[0..1];
   executionInstructions: order_execution::ClientExecutionInstruction[*];
}

// A quote is published by a venue, and the venue is order-core's.
Association order_execution::QuoteVenueLink
{
   quoteVenue: order_core::Venue[0..1];
   venueQuotes: order_execution::VenueQuote[*];
}

Association order_execution::PerformanceVenueLink
{
   performanceVenue: order_core::Venue[0..1];
   venuePerformances: order_execution::VenuePerformance[*];
}

// ---- crossing into trade-capture ----

// THE OTHER HALF OF THE CENTRAL LINK: the fill became this trade.
Association order_execution::BookingTradeLink
{
   bookedTrade: trade_capture::Trade[0..1];
   executionBookings: order_execution::TradeBooking[*];
}

Association order_execution::BookingBreakTradeLink
{
   breakTrade: trade_capture::Trade[0..1];
   executionBookingBreaks: order_execution::BookingBreak[*];
}

// The desk's split of a fill and the back office's allocation of a trade are two records of
// one decision, and they can disagree.
Association order_execution::AllocationTradeLink
{
   tradeAllocation: trade_capture::Allocation[0..1];
   executionFillAllocations: order_execution::FillAllocation[*];
}

// An explicit cost line has an invoice behind it; that invoice is a trade-capture charge.
Association order_execution::CostChargeLink
{
   sourceCharge: trade_capture::TradeCharge[0..1];
   executionCostLines: order_execution::ExecutionCostLine[*];
}

// The venue as the regulator knows it, so a performance row can be published under a MIC a
// client can look up. order-core and trade-capture each hold their own record of a venue and
// neither is authoritative, so a performance row joins to both.
Association order_execution::PerformanceMifidVenueLink
{
   mifidVenue: trade_capture::ExecutionVenue[0..1];
   venuePerformanceLines: order_execution::VenuePerformance[*];
}

// ---- internal ----

Association order_execution::FillReports
{
   reports: order_execution::ExecutionReport[*];
   reportFill: order_execution::Fill[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::FillAmendments
{
   fillAmendments: order_execution::FillAmendment[*];
   amendedFill: order_execution::Fill[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::FillAllocations
{
   fillAllocations: order_execution::FillAllocation[*];
   allocationFill: order_execution::Fill[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::FillAllocationSet
{
   allocationSet: order_execution::AllocationSet[0..1];
   setFill: order_execution::Fill[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::SetAllocations
{
   setAllocations: order_execution::FillAllocation[*];
   owningSet: order_execution::AllocationSet[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::SetBreaks
{
   allocationBreaks: order_execution::AllocationBreak[*];
   breakSet: order_execution::AllocationSet[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::SummaryFillLink
{
   summary: order_execution::OrderFillSummary[0..1];
   summaryFills: order_execution::Fill[*];
}

Association order_execution::FillBooking
{
   booking: order_execution::TradeBooking[0..1];
   bookingFill: order_execution::Fill[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::BookingBreaks
{
   bookingBreaks: order_execution::BookingBreak[*];
   breakBooking: order_execution::TradeBooking[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::FillCostLines
{
   costLines: order_execution::ExecutionCostLine[*];
   costFill: order_execution::Fill[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::FillSlippage
{
   slippageMeasures: order_execution::SlippageMeasure[*];
   slippageFill: order_execution::Fill[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::BenchmarkSlippage
{
   benchmarkSlippages: order_execution::SlippageMeasure[*];
   slippageBenchmark: order_execution::ExecutionBenchmark[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::FillImpact
{
   marketImpact: order_execution::MarketImpactEstimate[0..1];
   impactFill: order_execution::Fill[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::ImpactReversion
{
   reversions: order_execution::ReversionMeasure[*];
   reversionImpact: order_execution::MarketImpactEstimate[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::ShortfallArrival
{
   arrival: order_execution::ArrivalSnapshot[0..1];
   arrivalShortfalls: order_execution::ImplementationShortfall[*];
}

Association order_execution::FillQuote
{
   prevailingQuote: order_execution::VenueQuote[0..1];
   quotedFills: order_execution::Fill[*];
}

Association order_execution::FillQuoteComparison
{
   quoteComparison: order_execution::QuoteComparison[0..1];
   comparisonFill: order_execution::Fill[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::QuoteComparisons
{
   quoteComparisons: order_execution::QuoteComparison[*];
   comparedQuote: order_execution::VenueQuote[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::AssessmentFactors
{
   factors: order_execution::BestExecutionFactor[*];
   factorAssessment: order_execution::BestExecutionAssessment[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::AssessmentPolicy
{
   policy: order_execution::ExecutionPolicy[0..1];
   policyAssessments: order_execution::BestExecutionAssessment[*];
}

Association order_execution::AssessmentReport
{
   report: order_execution::BestExecutionReport[0..1];
   reportAssessments: order_execution::BestExecutionAssessment[*];
}

Association order_execution::ReportVenuePerformance
{
   reportVenuePerformance: order_execution::VenuePerformance[*];
   performanceReport: order_execution::BestExecutionReport[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::FillLiquidity
{
   liquidityCapture: order_execution::LiquidityCapture[0..1];
   captureFill: order_execution::Fill[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::FillSession
{
   session: order_execution::ExecutionSession[0..1];
   sessionFills: order_execution::Fill[*];
}

Association order_execution::SessionParticipation
{
   participationSession: order_execution::ExecutionSession[0..1];
   sessionParticipation: order_execution::ParticipationMeasure[*];
}

Association order_execution::FillLatency
{
   latency: order_execution::LatencyMeasure[0..1];
   latencyFill: order_execution::Fill[0..1];
}

Association order_execution::FillExceptions
{
   exceptions: order_execution::ExecutionException[*];
   exceptionFill: order_execution::Fill[0..1];
}
